tyle\",serif'>Test Name<\/span><\/u><\/b><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n  <td width=84 valign=top style='width:63.0pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:12.95pt'>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><b><u><span\r\n  lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>Result<\/span><\/u><\/b><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n  <td width=78 valign=top style='width:58.65pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:12.95pt'>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><b><u><span\r\n  lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>Unit<\/span><\/u><\/b><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n  <td width=245 valign=top style='width:183.45pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:12.95pt'>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal><b><u><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\r\n  \"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>Bio. Ref. Interval <\/span><\/u><\/b><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n <\/tr>\r\n <tr style='height:12.95pt'>\r\n  <td width=313 valign=top style='width:234.9pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:12.95pt'><\/td>\r\n  <td width=84 valign=top style='width:63.0pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:12.95pt'><\/td>\r\n  <td width=78 valign=top style='width:58.65pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:12.95pt'><\/td>\r\n  <td width=245 valign=top style='width:183.45pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:12.95pt'><\/td>\r\n <\/tr>\r\n <tr style='height:26.6pt'>\r\n  <td width=313 valign=top style='width:234.9pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:26.6pt'>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\r\n  \"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>NT-Pro BNP<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:1.7pt;line-height:6.9pt;text-autospace:\r\n  none'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>(<\/span><span\r\n  lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\n  color:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>Methodology: FIA<\/span><span lang=EN-US\r\n  style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>)<\/span><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n  <td width=84 valign=top style='width:63.0pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:26.6pt'>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><b><span\r\n  lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\n  color:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>1076.57<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n  <td width=78 valign=top style='width:58.65pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:26.6pt'>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><span lang=EN-US\r\n  style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;color:black;\r\n  letter-spacing:-.5pt'>pg\/mL<\/span><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n  <td width=245 valign=top style='width:183.45pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\r\n  height:26.6pt'>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\n  color:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>0.00 - 353<\/span><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n <\/tr>\r\n<\/table>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:5.4pt;line-height:5.4pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:7.0pt'>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:5.4pt;line-height:5.4pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>COMMENT<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:10.6pt;margin-right:14.4pt;margin-bottom:\r\n0cm;margin-left:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:9.0pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>BNP was initially called brain natriuretic\r\npeptide because it was first found in brain tissue (and to distinguish it from\r\na similar protein made in the atria, or upper chambers, of the heart, termed\r\nANP). BNP is actually produced primarily by cells in the left ventricle of the\r\nheart. The left ventricle is the heart's main pumping chamber and is\r\nresponsible for sending oxygenated blood from the lungs to the remainder of the\r\nbody. It is associated with blood volume and pressure and with the work that\r\nthe heart must do in pumping blood throughout the body. Small amounts of a\r\nprecursor protein, pro-BNP, are continuously produced by the heart. Pro-BNP is\r\nthen split by the enzyme called corin to release the active hormone BNP and an\r\ninactive fragment, NT-proBNP, into the blood. <\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:9.0pt;margin-right:14.4pt;margin-bottom:\r\n0cm;margin-left:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:9.1pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) and\r\nN-terminal pro b-type natriuretic peptide (NTproBNP) are peptide (small\r\nproteins) that are either hormones or part of the peptide that contained the\r\nhormone at one time. They are continually produced in small quantities in the\r\nheart and released in larger quantities when the heart senses that it needs to\r\nwork harder. This supports fluid retention and volume expansion in the arteries\r\nand veins. Subsequently, the heart muscle is stretched and works hard to pump\r\nblood under normal resting condition. Tests for BNP and NT-proBNP measure their\r\nlevels in the blood in order <\/span><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;\r\nfont-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;color:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>to\r\ndetect and evaluate heart failure.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:9.0pt;margin-right:14.4pt;margin-bottom:\r\n0cm;margin-left:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:9.1pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>A proper differential diagnosis is important,\r\nbecause NT-proBNP is also elevated in other conditions affecting ventricular\r\nfunction such as pulmonary embolism, hypertension, valvular heart disease\r\nmuscle heart disease, arrhythmias, critical illness (e.g. sepsis). anemia and\r\nstorke. In primary care, NTp-proBNP is particularly useful to guide referral of\r\nsymptomatic chronic HF to specialist care because it excludes suspected left\r\nventricular systolic dysfunction. NT-proBNP has independent prognostic value in\r\na variety of clinical settings, including acute dyspnea with or without acute\r\nHF<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:11.3pt;line-height:5.4pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>Clinical Uses<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:3.6pt;line-height:6.9pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>As an aid in the diagnosis of suspected cases\r\nof CHF.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:331.2pt;line-height:9.0pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>Detection of mild forms of cardiac\r\ndysfunction <\/span><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'><br>\r\n<\/span><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>To assess severity of heart failure in\r\nalready diagnosed cases of CHF. <\/span><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;\r\nfont-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'><br>\r\n<\/span><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>For risk stratification of patients with\r\nAcute Coronary Syndrome &amp; CHF.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:2.3pt;line-height:6.8pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>For monitoring therapy in patients with Left\r\nVentricular dysfunction.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:11.3pt;line-height:6.8pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>NYHA Heart failure Classification NT-proBNP\r\n(pg\/mL)<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:2.3pt;line-height:6.8pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>Negative\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 &lt;300<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:2.3pt;line-height:5.3pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>I Level\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 300-450<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:3.7pt;line-height:5.4pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>II Level\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 450-1700<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:3.7pt;line-height:5.4pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>III Level\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n1700-4200<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:3.7pt;line-height:5.3pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>IV Level\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 &gt;4200<\/span><\/b><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:3.7pt;line-height:5.3pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><b><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;\r\ncolor:black;letter-spacing:-.5pt'>Nevertheless, elevated NPs levels can be\r\nfound in many circumstances involving<\/span><\/b><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif;color:black;\r\nletter-spacing:-.5pt'>:<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>1.Left\r\nventricular (LV) dysfunction or hypertrophy;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>\u00a02.Right\r\nventricular (RV) dysfunction secondary to pulmonary diseases;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:3.7pt;line-height:5.3pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>\u00a03.Cardiac\r\ninflammatory or infectious diseases;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-top:3.7pt;line-height:5.3pt;text-autospace:\r\nnone'><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:8.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>&nbsp;<\/spa